WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.887
GPU rental is useful when flexibility matters more than ownership: temporary workloads, model evaluations, bursts, distributed team access, and specialized memory requirements.

00:00:10.887 --> 00:00:23.626
Choose among major providers, specialized clouds, marketplaces, notebooks, dedicated instances, and managed inference according to the control, support, interruption tolerance, and data boundary the job requires.

00:00:23.626 --> 00:00:27.101
Match the device to the workload.

00:00:27.101 --> 00:00:35.208
VRAM, memory bandwidth, GPU generation, software support, context, batch, concurrency, and multimodal inputs all influence fit.

00:00:35.208 --> 00:00:39.609
Several GPUs do not automatically behave as one.

00:00:39.609 --> 00:00:47.716
Test the smallest plausible configuration with real work and scale only when measurements identify a limit.

00:00:47.716 --> 00:00:50.264
Control the complete cost.

00:00:50.264 --> 00:00:56.518
Include provisioning, idle time, persistent storage, caches, snapshots, data transfer, and cleanup.

00:00:56.518 --> 00:01:02.309
Use commitments for predictable demand and interruptible capacity for restartable work.

00:01:02.309 --> 00:01:09.489
Budgets, alerts, ownership tags, automatic expiration, and shutdown automation turn good intentions into controls.

00:01:09.489 --> 00:01:19.912
Build a reproducible environment, protect SSH and API access, keep secrets out of images, and classify prompts and documents before upload.

00:01:19.912 --> 00:01:28.019
Monitor service health and hardware use, checkpoint important work, and preserve a rebuild and rollback path.

00:01:28.019 --> 00:01:38.443
When the job is complete, stop or delete compute and account for every remaining disk, snapshot, log, key, and network rule.

00:01:38.443 --> 00:01:49.329
Practical compute on demand means the rental is productive while active and leaves neither uncontrolled spending nor exposed data after it ends.
